  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The False King Bolete (Boletus huronensis) is a wood-decomposing fungus in the bolete family, distinguished by its robust appearance and somewhat deceptive resemblance to prized edible species. Despite its misleading common name, it is not poisonous but is considered unpalatable and not worth consuming.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color and Texture",
      "description": "Brown to dark brown cap with a somewhat velvety or matte surface, lacking the fine network pattern found on true King Boletes",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface Reaction",
      "description": "Yellow pores that bruise greenish or bluish when handled, a key distinguishing feature from edible lookalikes",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Characteristics",
      "description": "Stout stem with fine red or reddish reticulation (net-like pattern) in the upper portion, often fading lower down",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "False King Boletes fruit on or near decaying hardwood logs and stumps throughout New England forests, particularly in oak and beech woodlands.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'False King Bolete' warns foragers about its resemblance to the edible King Bolete (Boletus edulis), but the bluish bruising on its pores is an easy way to tell them apart in the field.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
